{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Settled the child question and opened the next measurement. The child question about handle_majority_promises is now explained with defect_claim refuted in its independent form: the candidate's post-sync accepted index equals the adopted promise's accepted count whenever the resume point lies inside that promise's content (the suffix supplies the remaining entries and the carried StopSign's +1 cancels against the one the adopted promise already counted), and equals the resume point plus that term otherwise, so a disagreement between the adopted decided index and the entries held would need a promise reporting a decided index above the adopted promise's content — excluded for truthful reports by the invariant this code relies on instead of a local clamp, and not produced by the observed corrupted reports either; a fact records that identity and the question is dispositioned explained with a residual discriminator naming the only reopening case. This turn's action is a new experiment testing whether the witnessed inflation compounds: the harness gains a repeated-resynchronization case that performs three rounds of the two-follower reconnect-and-drain sequence after the StopSign is decided and samples all three nodes after every round under unique event names, with a baseline comparison (six indices of content, StopSign reported as decided, six-element decided read) admitted from the prefix and result comparisons requiring, after each round, that re-synchronized replicas still report at most the six indices they hold, that their decided-log read still has six elements, and that the leader still reports six decided indices covered by its entries. The rationale states the prediction: since each re-synchronization adds one index and the leader's phantom decision follows the inflated reports, the reported accepted and decided indices should keep growing while the content stays fixed, which would turn a one-off shift into an unbounded drift. Validation passes and execution is requested; incomplete are that execution's result, the parent's remaining limits (loss- and timer-triggered re-synchronization, larger clusters, persistence across restarts, compaction calls) and any further independent lead.\"}"}}
